@article{ WOS:001253855400001,
Author = {Arandjelovic, Mimi and Stephens, Colleen R. and Dieguez, Paula and
   Maldonado, Nuria and Bocksberger, Gaelle and Despres-Einspenner,
   Marie-Lyne and Debetencourt, Benjamin and Estienne, Vittoria and Kalan,
   Ammie K. and Mccarthy, Maureen S. and Granjon, Anne-Celine and Staedele,
   Veronika and Harder, Briana and Hacker, Lucia and Landsmann, Anja and
   Lynn, Laura K. and Pfund, Heidi and Rockaiova, Zuzana and Sigler,
   Kristeena and Widness, Jane and Wilken, Heike and Buzharevski, Antonio
   and Goffe, Adeelia S. and Havercamp, Kristin and Luncz, Lydia L. and
   Sirianni, Giulia and Wessling, Erin G. and Wittig, Roman M. and Boesch,
   Christophe and Kuehl, Hjalmar S.},
Title = {Highly precise community science annotations of video camera-trapped
   fauna in challenging environments},
Journal = {REMOTE SENSING IN ECOLOGY AND CONSERVATION},
Year = {2024},
Volume = {10},
Number = {6},
Pages = {702-724},
Month = {DEC},
Abstract = {As camera trapping grows in popularity and application, some analytical
   limitations persist including processing time and accuracy of data
   annotation. Typically images are recorded by camera traps although
   videos are becoming increasingly collected even though they require much
   more time for annotation. To overcome limitations with image annotation,
   camera trap studies are increasingly linked to community science (CS)
   platforms. Here, we extend previous work on CS image annotations to
   camera trap videos from a challenging environment; a dense tropical
   forest with low visibility and high occlusion due to thick canopy cover
   and bushy undergrowth at the camera level. Using the CS platform
   Chimp\&See, established for classification of 599 956 video clips from
   tropical Africa, we assess annotation precision and accuracy by
   comparing classification of 13 531 1-min video clips by a professional
   ecologist (PE) with output from 1744 registered, as well as
   unregistered, Chimp\&See community scientists. We considered 29
   classification categories, including 17 species and 12 higher-level
   categories, in which phenotypically similar species were grouped.
   Overall, annotation precision was 95.4\%, which increased to 98.2\% when
   aggregating similar species groups together. Our findings demonstrate
   the competence of community scientists working with camera trap videos
   from even challenging environments and hold great promise for future
   studies on animal behaviour, species interaction dynamics and population
   monitoring.
   Alors que le pi \& eacute;geage photographique gagne en popularit \&
   eacute; et en application, certaines limites analytiques persistent,
   notamment le temps de traitement et la pr \& eacute;cision de
   l'annotation des donn \& eacute;es. Les images statiques sont le plus
   souvent enregistr \& eacute;es par les pi \& egrave;ges photographiques,
   mais la collecte de vid \& eacute;os est de plus en plus r \&
   eacute;pandue, m \& ecirc;me si l'annotation des donn \& eacute;es prend
   beaucoup plus de temps. Pour surmonter les limitations li \& eacute;es
   \& agrave; l'annotation des images, les \& eacute;tudes sur les pi \&
   egrave;ges photographiques sont de plus en plus souvent associ \&
   eacute;es aux plateformes de sciences communautaires (SC). Ici, nous \&
   eacute;tendons les travaux pr \& eacute;c \& eacute;dents sur les
   annotations d'images par la SC aux vid \& eacute;os de pi \& egrave;ges
   photographiques provenant d'un environnement difficile : une for \&
   ecirc;t tropicale dense avec une faible visibilit \& eacute; et une
   forte obstruction visuelle en raison de l'\& eacute;paisse couverture de
   la canop \& eacute;e et des sous-bois buissonneux au niveau de la cam \&
   eacute;ra. En utilisant la plateforme de SC Chimp\&See, \& eacute;tablie
   pour la classification de 599 956 clips vid \& eacute;o d'Afrique
   tropicale, nous \& eacute;valuons la pr \& eacute;cision et l'exactitude
   des annotations en comparant la classification de 13 531 clips vid \&
   eacute;o d'une dur \& eacute;e d'une minute par un \& eacute;cologiste
   professionnel avec les r \& eacute;sultats de 1 744 scientifiques
   communautaires enregistr \& eacute;s sur Chimp\&See, en plus de ceux
   non-enregistr \& eacute;s. Nous avons pris en compte 29 cat \&
   eacute;gories de classification, comprenant 17 esp \& egrave;ces et 12
   cat \& eacute;gories de niveau sup \& eacute;rieur, dans lesquelles les
   esp \& egrave;ces ph \& eacute;notypiquement similaires ont \& eacute;t
   \& eacute; regroup \& eacute;es. Dans l'ensemble, la pr \& eacute;cision
   des annotations \& eacute;tait de 95,4 \%, et de 98,2 \% lorsque les
   groupes d'esp \& egrave;ces similaires \& eacute;taient regroup \&
   eacute;s. Nos r \& eacute;sultats d \& eacute;montrent la comp \&
   eacute;tence des scientifiques communautaires travaillant avec des vid
   \& eacute;os issues de pi \& egrave;ges photographiques, m \& ecirc;me
   dans des environnements difficiles, et sont tr \& egrave;s prometteurs
   pour les \& eacute;tudes futures sur le comportement des animaux, la
   dynamique des interactions entre les esp \& egrave;ces et le suivi des
   populations.
   Even though they require more time for annotation compared to still
   images, camera trap videos provide valuable acoustic and visual
   information on species' behaviour and can improve identification of
   individuals. Here we use Community Science (CS) to annotate camera trap
   videos from a challenging environment, a dense tropical forest with low
   visibility and high occlusion. Using the CS platform Chimp\&See, we
   assess annotation precision and accuracy by comparing classification of
   13 531 1-min video clips by a professional ecologist with 1744
   Chimp\&See community scientists. Overall, annotation precision was
   between 95.4 and 98.1\%. Our findings demonstrate the competence of
   community scientists with challenging videos and hold great promise for
   future studies on animal behaviour and population monitoring. image},
